## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that won't work on .NET 8:

| API | Problem on .NET 8 |
|-----|-------------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` without cancellation | Can't be cooperatively cancelled |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            // GetConsumingEnumerable blocks until an item is available
            // and throws OperationCanceledException when token is cancelled
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();               // wakes the blocked thread cooperatively
        _thread?.Join(5000);         // wait for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Key Changes

1. **`BlockingCollection<string>`** replaces `Queue` + `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` — it's thread-safe and supports cancellation natively.
2. **`CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** replaces both `Thread.Interrupt()` and `Thread.Abort()` — it wakes blocked `GetConsumingEnumerable` immediately.
3. **No fallback kill needed** — cooperative cancellation means the thread exits its loop cleanly.

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| `Handle(item)` runs a long operation that ignores cancellation | Pass `_cts.Token` into `Handle` and check it at checkpoints |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Implement `IDisposable` as shown |
| Calling `Enqueue` after `Stop` | `BlockingCollection.Add` throws `InvalidOperationException` if you call `CompleteAdding()` — decide if you need that guard |
| Assuming `Stop()` returns instantly | The thread finishes the current `Handle(item)` before exiting; the 5s join timeout still applies |

## If You Need the Manual Approach (no `BlockingCollection`)

```csharp
private void RunLoop()
{
    while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // WaitAny unblocks on either the event OR cancellation
        int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(
            new[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle });

        if (index == 1) break; // cancellation signaled

        _newEvent.Reset();
        string item;
        lock (_lock) { item = _queue.Dequeue(); }
        Handle(item);
    }
}
```

This uses `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `_cts.Token.WaitHandle` to replace the interrupt/abort wake-up mechanism.
